ution of the Pell equation x\u00b2 -6y\u00b2=1.\n\nSo, the solutions can be generated as:\n\nx + b\u221a6 = (3 + \u221a6)(5 + 2\u221a6)^k, where k is a non-negative integer.\n\nLet me verify this.\n\nFirst, for k=0:\n\n(3 + \u221a6)(5 + 2\u221a6)^0 = 3 + \u221a6\n\nThen x=3, b=1, which is the fundamental solution.\n\nFor k=1:\n\nMultiply (3 + \u221a6)(5 + 2\u221a6):\n\nFirst, compute (3)(5) + 3(2\u221a6) + \u221a6(5) + \u221a6(2\u221a6)\n\n=15 + 6\u221a6 +5\u221a6 + 2*6\n\n=15 + 11\u221a6 +12\n\n=27 + 11\u221a6\n\nSo x=27, b=11.\n\nCheck x\u00b2 -6b\u00b2=27\u00b2 -6*(11)^2=729 -6*121=729 -726=3. Correct.\n\nSo next solution is x=27, b=11. Then since x=3a, we have a=27/3=9. So a=9, which was the solution we found earlier. Then n=(a\u00b2 -1)/2=(81-1)/2=40. So that's the n=40 case.\n\nNext, k=2:\n\nMultiply the previous solution (27 +11\u221a6) by (5 +2\u221a6):\n\n(27 +11\u221a6)(5 +2\u221a6) =27*5 +27*2\u221a6 +11\u221a6*5 +11\u221a6*2\u221a6\n\n=135 +54\u221a6 +55\u221a6 +22*6\n\n=135 +109\u221a6 +132\n\n=267 +109\u221a6\n\nThus x=267, b=109. Check x\u00b2 -6b\u00b2:\n\n267\u00b2 -6*109\u00b2 =71289 -6*11881=71289 -71286=3. Correct.\n\nSo x=267, which is 3a=267 => a=89. Then n=(89\u00b2 -1)/2=(7921 -1)/2=7920/2=3960. Let me check 3n +1=3*3960 +1=11881, which is 109\u00b2. Correct. So n=3960. But 3960 is clearly not a prime number. So this gives a larger n, but still composite.\n\nSimilarly, the next solution would be even larger. For k=3:\n\nMultiply (267 +109\u221a6)(5 +2\u221a6):\n\n267*5 +267*2\u221a6 +109\u221a6*5 +109\u221a6*2\u221a6\n\n=1335 +534\u221a6 +545\u221a6 +218*6\n\n=1335 +1079\u221a6 +1308\n\n=2643 +1079\u221a6\n\nSo x=2643, b=1079. Then a=2643/3=881. Then n=(881\u00b2 -1)/2=(776161 -1)/2=776160/2=388080. 3n +1=3*388080 +1=1164241=1079\u00b2. Correct. Again, n=388080 is not prime.\n\nSo the solutions for n are 40, 3960, 388080, etc., each time getting larger. All of these n are even numbers. Wait, let's check n=40: 40 is even. n=3960: even. n=38808