ardless of the third person. The second group is specifically PB, PC, MP without CM. So yes, disjoint.\n\nTherefore, total teams covering all four subjects:354.\n\nNow, compute teams covering exactly three subjects.\n\nTo cover exactly three subjects, the team must be missing one subject. There are four possible subjects to miss. For each subject, calculate the number of teams that don't include that subject.\n\nBut since the team is composed of people who each know two subjects, it's possible that even if no one knows a particular subject, the team might still cover it through other subjects. Wait, no. If the team doesn't have anyone who knows a subject, then they don't cover it. But the problem is that each person knows two subjects, so missing a subject means that none of the team members have that subject in their pair.\n\nWait, for example, to miss mathematics, the team must consist of people who only know physics, chemistry, and biology. But looking at the groups:\n\n- MP: knows M and P,\n\n- PC: knows P and C,\n\n- CM: knows C and M,\n\n- PB: knows P and B.\n\nSo, if we want a team that doesn't cover mathematics, none of the team members can be from MP or CM (since those groups include mathematics). Therefore, such a team can only consist of people from PC and PB.\n\nSimilarly, to miss chemistry, the team cannot include anyone from PC or CM.\n\nTo miss physics, the team cannot include anyone from MP, PC, or PB (since all those groups include physics). But CM is the only group without physics. But CM knows C and M. So a team missing physics would have to consist only of CM members. But CM has 3 people. So the number of teams missing physics is C(3,3)=1. But we already considered these as invalid teams (all within CM), which were subtracted earlier.\n\nSimilarly, to miss biology, the team cannot include anyone from PB. So the team can be formed from MP, PC, CM.\n\nBut let's go step by step.\n\nTotal teams covering exactly three subjects = sum over each subject of the number of teams that don't include that subject, minus overlaps. But this is complex.\n\nAlternatively, since we already calculated teams covering all four subjects (354), and we know the total valid teams (1080), then teams covering exactly three subjects would be 1080 - 354=726. But this is if our initial calculation is correct. But we need to verify.\n\nBut let's try to compute it directly.\n\nFor each subject, compute the number of teams that do not include that subject. Then use inclusion-exclusion to find the number of teams missing at least one subject, but since we want exactly three subjects, it's tricky.\n\nWait, another approach: The number of teams that cover exactly three subjects is equal to the sum over each combination of three subjects of the number of teams that cover exactly those three subjects.\n\nThere are C(4,3)=4 combinations of three subjects.\n\n1. M, P, C\n\n2. M, P, B\n\n3. M, C, B\n\n4. P, C, B\n\nBut let's check if each of these combinations is possible.\n\nFor example, to cover M, P, C: the team must include people who can cover M, P, and C. Since individuals know two subjects, possible combinations.\n\nSimilarly for the others.\n\nBut biology is only in PB, so any team covering B must include PB. Therefore, the combination M, C, B requires PB (for B) and someone with M and C, which is CM. So a team with CM and PB would cover M, C, B. Similarly, they might need a third person, but wait, two people can cover M, C, B: CM (C, M) and PB (P, B). Wait, but PB also brings P. So if we have CM and PB, they cover C, M, P, B. That's all four subjects. Therefore, to cover exactly M, C, B, we need a team that doesn't include P. But since PB includes P, and CM includes M and C, if we have CM and PB, we get P as well. Therefore, it's impossible to cover M, C, B without also covering P. Therefore, the combination M, C, B is impossible to cover exactly, because any team covering M and B (through PB) would also have P. Therefore, the combination 