ints in general position with no empty convex k-gon. But in our case, the problem is slightly different: we need that every convex pentagon has at least one point inside. So, we are to prevent empty convex pentagons. So, perhaps the minimal number of points required to ensure that no empty convex pentagon exists, given that the convex hull is a heptagon.\n\nBut Erd\u0151s-type problems usually focus on existence. Here, we have a conditional: if five points form a convex pentagon, then there's a point inside. So, this is like saying the set M is 5-empty-convex-pentagon-free, but with a convex hull of size 7.\n\nI need to think about configurations. Let me start with the convex heptagon. If all the points are on the convex hull, then obviously there are many empty convex pentagons (any five consecutive vertices would form an empty convex pentagon). So, we need to add interior points to block these.\n\nBut how many interior points are needed? If we can place points such that every potential convex pentagon (either from the hull or combining hull and interior points) contains at least one interior point.\n\nOne strategy is to place points in such a way that any convex pentagon must contain at least one interior point. Maybe using a grid or some structured placement. But perhaps a more efficient way is to use a smaller convex polygon inside the heptagon such that any convex pentagon must enclose it.\n\nWait, if we place a convex hexagon inside the heptagon, then maybe a convex pentagon formed by five outer points might not necessarily contain the inner hexagon. Hmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, if we place a single point inside the heptagon. Then, can we ensure that every convex pentagon contains this point? Probably not. For example, take five consecutive vertices of the heptagon; their convex hull is a convex pentagon, which might not contain the single interior point unless it's placed in a specific location. But depending on the position of the single point, maybe som