erating functions and roots of unity, the number of non-empty subsets of A={1,2,...,10} whose sum is a multiple of 10 is 103.", "deepseek_solution": "To determine the number of non-empty subsets of \\( A = \\{1, 2, \\ldots, 10\\} \\) whose sum is a multiple of 10, we use generating functions and roots of unity. \n\n**Step-by-Step Process:**\n\n1. **Generating Function Setup:**\n   The generating function for subset sums is:\n   \\[\n   G(x) = (1 + x^1)(1 + x^2) \\cdots (1 + x^{10})\n   \\]\n   The coefficient of \\( x^k \\) in \\( G(x) \\) gives the number of subsets with sum \\( k \\).\n\n2. **Roots of Unity Filter:**\n   To extract coefficients corresponding to multiples of 10, we use the roots of unity filter:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Number of subsets} = \\frac{1}{10} \\sum_{k=0}^{9} G(e^{2\\pi i k/10})\n   \\]\n   This includes the empty set, so we subtract 1 for the final count.\n\n3. **Evaluating \\( G \\) at Roots of Unity:**\n   - **At \\( k = 0 \\):** \\( G(1) = 2^{10} = 1024 \\).\n   - **At \\( k = 5 \\):** \\( G(-1) = 0 \\) since a term in the product becomes zero.\n   - **For \\( k = 1, 3, 7, 9 \\):** Each evaluation results in 0 due to the product cycling through all 10th roots of unity and including a zero term.\n   - **For \\( k = 2, 4, 6, 8 \\):** Each pair (e.g., \\( k = 2 \\) and \\( k = 8 \\)) contributes 4, summing to 8 for each pair.\n\n4. **Summing Contributions:**\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{10} \\left(1024 + 0 + 8 + 0 + 8 + 0 \\right) = \\frac{1040}{10} = 104\n   \\]\n   Subtracting 1 for the empty set gives \\( 104 - 1 = 103 \\).\n\n**Final Answer:**\n\\[\n\\boxed{103}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n\nTo solve this problem, we need to identify all subsets of the set \\( A = \\{1,2, \\ldots, 10\\} \\) in which the sum of the elements is a multiple of 10. We will refer to these subsets as \"good subsets.\" We shall systematically examine the subsets whose sums are multiples of 10.\n\n1. Sum equals 10:\n   - Identify all combinations of elements in \\