uld create a new 5-digit subsequence \\( Q0 \\) or \\( Q1 \\) (where \\( Q \\) is the last four digits). By property 2, both \\( Q0 \\) and \\( Q1 \\) must already exist in the sequence.\n\n2. **Existence of \\( Q0 \\) and \\( Q1 \\)**:  \n   Since \\( Q0 \\) and \\( Q1 \\) are present in the sequence, the quartet \\( Q \\) must appear at least twice: once followed by 0 and once by 1. The final occurrence of \\( Q \\) is at the end of the sequence, not followed by any digit.\n\n3. **Graph Representation (de Bruijn Graph)**:  \n   Represent the sequence as a path in a directed graph where nodes are 4-digit sequences, and edges are 5-digit sequences. Each edge connects nodes via overlapping 4 digits. For the sequence to be maximal, the last node \\( Q \\) must have both outgoing edges (corresponding to \\( Q0 \\) and \\( Q1 \\)) already traversed, implying \\( Q \\) was previously visited.\n\n4. **Cyclical Structure**:  \n   To revisit \\( Q \\) after its first occurrence, the sequence must form a cycle. In a linear sequence, this is only possible if the sequence starts and ends with \\( Q \\). Thus, the first 4 digits (initial node) and last 4 digits (final node) must coincide to enable such a cycle without repeating edges.\n\n**Conclusion**:  \nThe first 4 digits equal the last 4 digits to satisfy the maximality and uniqueness constraints, forming a structure analogous to a cyclic de Bruijn sequence truncated to a linear maximal path. Hence, the first four digits are identical to the last four digits. \n\n**Answer**:  \nThe first four digits match the last four digits to maintain the sequence's maximal uniqueness, ensuring no extension is possible without repeating a 5-digit subsequence. Thus, the first and last four digits must be identical.  \n\\boxed{\\text{The first 4 digits are identical to the last 4 digits.}}", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Define and Assume the Sequence:**\n   Let \\( \\{ a_n \\} \\) be the given finite sequence of 0s and 1s with properti