ly the entire real line. But since our function f maps [0, \u221e) to [0,1), which is a subset of [0, \u221e), and is increasing, the composition f(f(f(x))) is also increasing. Therefore, the equation x = f(f(f(x))) can only intersect the line y=x at points where the function f has fixed points. Since f is increasing and the composition is also increasing, if there was another intersection, it would have to correspond to another fixed point of f, but we already found all fixed points of f(x) which are 0 and 0.5.\n\nTherefore, the only real solutions are the symmetric ones: (0,0,0) and (0.5, 0.5, 0.5).\n\nTo be thorough, let's suppose there exists a solution where variables are not equal. Let's assume that x, y, z are distinct.\n\nSuppose x \u2260 y \u2260 z \u2260 x. Let's try to see if such a solution is possible.\n\nFrom the first equation: y = 4x\u00b2/(1 +4x\u00b2). Since x is a real number, y is non-negative and less than 1. Similarly, z = 4y\u00b2/(1 +4y\u00b2), so z is also non-negative and less than 1. Then x = 4z\u00b2/(1 +4z\u00b2), so x is non-negative and less than 1. Therefore, all variables are in [0,1).\n\nSuppose x > 0.5. Let's see if that's possible. Suppose x = 0.6. Then y = 4*(0.6)^2/(1 +4*(0.6)^2) = 4*0.36/(1 + 2.44) = 1.44/3.44 \u22480.418. Then z = 4*(0.418)^2/(1 +4*(0.418)^2). Compute 0.418\u00b2 \u22480.174, 4*0.174\u22480.696. Denominator 1 +0.696\u22481.696. So z\u22480.696/1.696\u22480.410. Then x = 4*(0.410)^2/(1 +4*(0.410)^2). 0.410\u00b2\u22480.168, 4*0.168\u22480.672. Denominator\u22481.672. So x\u22480.672/1.672\u22480.402. So now x has decreased to 0.402. Then y would be \u22484*(0.402)^2/(1 +4*(0.402)^2). 0.402\u00b2\u22480.1616, 4*0.1616\u22480.646. Denominator\u22481.646. So y\u22480.646/1.646\u22480.392. Then z\u22484*(0.392)^2/(1 +4*(0.392)^2). 0.392\u00b2\u22480.1536, 4*0.1536\u22480.614. Denominator\u22481.614. So z\u22480.614/1.614\u22480.380. Then x\u22484*(0.380)^2/(1 +4*(0.380)^2). 0.38\u00b2\u22480.1444, 4*0.1444\u22480.5