.5, say x=0.49.\n\ny=4*(0.49)^2/(1 +4*(0.49)^2). 0.49\u00b2=0.2401, 4*0.2401=0.9604, denominator=1 +0.9604=1.9604. So y\u22480.9604/1.9604\u22480.49.\n\nSimilarly, z\u22480.49, and x\u22480.49. So near x=0.5, the values stay near 0.5. So x=0.5 is a stable fixed point, while x=0 is also a fixed point. If we start near zero, we approach zero. If we start above zero but not exactly at 0.5, we approach 0.5. Wait, but in our previous example starting at x=0.6, the iterations decreased towards 0.5. Wait, no, when we started at x=0.6, we got y\u22480.418, z\u22480.410, x\u22480.402, which is decreasing below 0.5. But then in the next iteration, x\u22480.402, which would lead to y\u22484*(0.402)^2/(1 +4*(0.402)^2)\u22484*0.1616/(1 +1.646)\u22480.646/2.646\u22480.244. Wait, no, previously when I did x=0.6, I think I made a miscalculation. Let me recast that.\n\nWait, let's redo the iteration starting from x=0.6:\n\nFirst iteration:\n\nx1 =0.6\n\ny1 =4*(0.6)^2/(1 +4*(0.6)^2)=4*0.36/(1 +2.44)=1.44/3.44\u22480.418\n\nz1=4*(0.418)^2/(1 +4*(0.418)^2)=4*(0.174)/ (1 +1.74)=0.696/2.74\u22480.254\n\nx2=4*(0.254)^2/(1 +4*(0.254)^2)=4*0.0645/(1 +0.258)=0.258/1.258\u22480.205\n\nThen y2=4*(0.205)^2/(1 +4*(0.205)^2)=4*0.042/(1 +0.168)=0.168/1.168\u22480.144\n\nz2=4*(0.144)^2/(1 +4*(0.144)^2)=4*0.0207/(1 +0.0828)=0.0828/1.0828\u22480.0765\n\nx3=4*(0.0765)^2/(1 +4*(0.0765)^2)=4*0.00585/(1 +0.0234)=0.0234/1.0234\u22480.0229\n\nContinuing:\n\ny3\u22484*(0.0229)^2/(1 +4*(0.0229)^2)\u22484*0.000524/1.0021\u22480.0021/1.0021\u22480.0021\n\nz3\u22484*(0.0021)^2/(1 +4*(0.0021)^2)\u22480.00001764/1.00001764\u22480.00001764\n\nx4\u22484*(0.00001764)/(1 +4*(0.00001764))\u22480.00007056/1.00007056\u22480.0000705\n\nSo, this sequence is rapidly approaching zero. Hence, starting from x=0.6, which is above 0.5, the iterations actually decrease below 0.5 and then rapidly approach zero. That's contradictory to the previous thought that near 0.5 it's stable. Wait, this suggests that x=0.5 is unstable? Beca