tion \\(x = f(f(f(x)))\\). Analyzing the behavior of \\(f\\) and its compositions, we find that \\(f(f(f(x)))\\) is increasing and bounded above by approximately 0.7191. The line \\(y = x\\) intersects this curve only at \\(x = 0\\) and \\(x = 0.5\\), indicating no other fixed points.\n\nConsidering potential cycles of period 3, we note that due to the monotonicity and boundedness of \\(f\\), such cycles cannot exist. Numerical iterations starting from various points show convergence to either 0 or 0.5, reinforcing that no non-symmetric solutions exist.\n\nThus, the only real number solutions are:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(0, 0, 0)} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\boxed{\\left( \\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{2} \\right)}.\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\nGiven the system of equations\n\\[\n\\left\\{\n\\begin{array}{l}\n\\frac{4x^{2}}{1 + 4x^{2}} = y, \\\\\n\\frac{4y^{2}}{1 + 4y^{2}} = z, \\\\\n\\frac{4z^{2}}{1 + 4z^{2}} = x.\n\\end{array}\n\\right.\n\\]\n\nWe need to find all real solutions and validate their correctness.\n\n1. **Assumption \\( x = y \\)**\n\n   If we assume \\( x = y \\), then from the first equation:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{4x^2}{1 + 4x^2} = x \\Rightarrow 4x^2 = x + 4x^4.\n   \\]\n   Rearranging terms:\n   \\[\n   4x^4 - 4x^2 + x = 0 \\Rightarrow x(4x^3 - 4x + 1) = 0.\n   \\]\n   This gives two possible solutions:\n   \\[\n   x = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad 4x^3 - 4x + 1 = 0.\n   \\]\n\n   Solving the cubic equation:\n   \\[\n   4x^3 - 4x + 1 = 0.\n   \\]\n   By trial and error or factorization, we find:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{1}{2}.\n   \\]\n   Since \\( x = y = z \\), we have two potential solutions:\n   \\[\n   (x, y, z) = (0, 0, 0) \\quad \\text{and} \\quad (x, y, z) = \\left( \\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{2} \\right).\n   \\]\n\n2. **Assumption \\( x \\neq y \\)** to show contradictions:\n\n   Assuming without loss of generality \\( x > y \\). From the cyclic nature and symmetry in the equations:\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{4x^2}{1 + 4x^2} < x \\quad \\text{implies} \\quad z > x.\n   \\]\n\n   Similarly:\n   \\[\n   z = \\frac{4y^2}{1 + 4y^2} < y,\n   \\]\n   which implies:\n   \\[\n   y > z.\n   \\]\n   This would lead to \\( x > y > z \\) and \\( z > x \\), a contradiction since \\( x > y > z > x \\) is not possible.\n\n3. **Verify non-negative solutions \\( x, y, z \\geq 0 \\):**\n\n   Clearly, \\( (0, 0, 0) \\) is a solution.\n\n   When \\( x, y, z > 0 \\), adding the three equations:\n   \\[\n   x + y + z = \\frac{4x^2}{1 + 4x^2} + \\frac{4y^2}{1 + 4y^2}+\\frac{4z^2}{1 + 4z^2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\( \\frac{4x^2}{1 + 4x^2} \\leq x \\), summing over the three terms gives:\n   \\[\n   x + y + z \\leq x + y + z.\n   \\]\n\n   By equality condition which only holds when:\n   \\[\n   x = y = z = \\frac{1}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Verifying:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{1}{2}, \\quad y = \\frac{1}{2}, \\quad z = \\frac{1}{2},\n   \\]\n   is indeed a solution.\n\n### Conclusion:\nThe system has exactly two real solutions:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(0, 0, 0) \\text{ and } \\left( \\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{2} \\right)}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\( p(x) = x^n + a_1 x^{n-1} + \\cdots + a_{n-1} x + a_n \\) and \\( g(x) = x^n + b_1 x^{n-1} + \\cdots + b_{n-1} x + b_n \\) be two polynomials with complex coefficients. The roots of \\( g(x) \\) are the squares of the roots of \\( p(x) \\). If \\( a_1 + a_3 + a_5 + \\cdots \\) and \\( a_2 + a_4 + a_6 + \\cdots \\) are real numbers, prove that \\( b_1 + b_2 + b_3 + \\cdots + b_n \\) is also a real number.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that if we have two polynomials, p(x) and g(x), where the roots of g(x) are the squares of the roots of p(x), and given that the sum of the odd-indexed coefficients (a1 + a3 + a5 + ...) and the sum of the even-indexed coefficients (a2 + a4 + a6 + ...) of p(x) are real numbers, then the sum of all coefficients of g(x) (b1 + b2 + ... + bn) is also real