os \u03b8_k - i sin \u03b8_k) = a cos \u03b8_k + b sin \u03b8_k + i(b cos \u03b8_k - a sin \u03b8_k). The real part is a cos \u03b8_k + b sin \u03b8_k. Then multiplying by e^{i\u03b8_k} gives (a cos \u03b8_k + b sin \u03b8_k)(cos \u03b8_k + i sin \u03b8_k) = (a cos \u03b8_k + b sin \u03b8_k) cos \u03b8_k + i(a cos \u03b8_k + b sin \u03b8_k) sin \u03b8_k, which matches the x'_k and y'_k coordinates above. So yes, this is correct.\n\nTherefore, the foot of the perpendicular P_k is given by Re(z e^{-i\u03b8_k}) e^{i\u03b8_k} = (a cos \u03b8_k + b sin \u03b8_k) e^{i\u03b8_k}.\n\nSo, in complex terms, P_k = (Re(z e^{-i\u03b8_k})) e^{i\u03b8_k}\n\nBut let's express z in polar coordinates. Let me suppose that z has magnitude r and argument \u03c6, so z = r e^{i\u03c6}. Then:\n\nP_k = Re(r e^{i\u03c6} e^{-i\u03b8_k}) e^{i\u03b8_k} = Re(r e^{i(\u03c6 - \u03b8_k)}) e^{i\u03b8_k} = [r cos(\u03c6 - \u03b8_k)] e^{i\u03b8_k}\n\nTherefore, P_k = r cos(\u03c6 - \u03b8_k) e^{i\u03b8_k}\n\nHmm, interesting. So each P_k is a complex number of magnitude |r cos(\u03c6 - \u03b8_k)| and angle \u03b8_k. But since M is inside the circle, r < R. However, the magnitude of each P_k is |r cos(\u03c6 - \u03b8_k)|. But since r is fixed and \u03b8_k varies, these magnitudes vary. Wait, but how can these points lie on a regular polygon if their magnitudes vary? Unless... their angles are arranged in a way that when plotted, they form a regular polygon.\n\nWait, but a regular polygon requires that all the vertices are equidistant from the center and equally spaced in angle. But here, each P_k has angle \u03b8_k, which is k\u03c0/n, but their magnitudes are r cos(\u03c6 - \u03b8_k). So unless r cos(\u03c6 - \u03b8_k) is constant for all k, which would only happen if \u03c6 - \u03b8_k is constant, which is impossible because \u03b8_k varies. So perhaps my initial approach is wrong.\n\nWait, but maybe there's another way. Let's see. If we consider all P_k, each is a point on the diameter at angle \u03b