ven as the intersections of the line through the focus with slope \\( k \\) and the parabola. Their coordinates were found in part (1). Let me recall their coordinates:\n\nPoint \\( A \\):\n\n\\( x_1 = \\frac{p(k^2 + 2 + 2\\sqrt{k^2 + 1})}{2k^2} \\)\n\n\\( y_1 = \\frac{p}{k}(1 + \\sqrt{k^2 + 1}) \\)\n\nPoint \\( B \\):\n\n\\( x_2 = \\frac{p(k^2 + 2 - 2\\sqrt{k^2 + 1})}{2k^2} \\)\n\n\\( y_2 = \\frac{p}{k}(1 - \\sqrt{k^2 + 1}) \\)\n\nSo, we need to find a point \\( C = (x, y) \\) on the parabola such that:\n\n1. \\( (x - x_A)^2 + (y - y_A)^2 = (x - x_B)^2 + (y - y_B)^2 \\) (equality of distances from \\( C \\) to \\( A \\) and \\( B \\))\n2. \\( (x - x_A)(x - x_B) + (y - y_A)(y - y_B) = 0 \\) (perpendicularity of vectors \\( \\vec{CA} \\) and \\( \\vec{CB} \\))\n\nAlternatively, the second condition can be written as:\n\n\\( \\vec{CA} \\cdot \\vec{CB} = 0 \\)\n\nLet me first write down both conditions.\n\nFirst, the distance equality:\n\n\\( (x - x_A)^2 + (y - y_A)^2 = (x - x_B)^2 + (y - y_B)^2 \\)\n\nExpanding both sides:\n\nLeft side:\n\n\\( x^2 - 2x x_A + x_A^2 + y^2 - 2y y_A + y_A^2 \\)\n\nRight side:\n\n\\( x^2 - 2x x_B + x_B^2 + y^2 - 2y y_B + y_B^2 \\)\n\nSubtract right side from left side:\n\n\\( (-2x x_A + x_A^2 - 2y y_A + y_A^2) - (-2x x_B + x_B^2 - 2y y_B + y_B^2) = 0 \\)\n\nSimplify:\n\n\\( -2x(x_A - x_B) + (x_A^2 - x_B^2) - 2y(y_A - y_B) + (y_A^2 - y_B^2) = 0 \\)\n\nFactor differences of squares:\n\n\\( -2x(x_A - x_B) + (x_A - x_B)(x_A + x_B) - 2y(y_A - y_B) + (y_A - y_B)(y_A + y_B) = 0 \\)\n\nFactor out \\( (x_A - x_B) \\) and \\( (y_A - y_B) \\):\n\n\\( (x_A - x_B)(-2x + x_A + x_B) + (y_A - y_B)(-2y + y_A + y_B) = 0 \\)\n\nLet me note that \\( x_A - x_B = \\frac{2p\\sqrt{k^2 + 1}}{k^2} \\) and \\( y_A - y_B = \\frac{2p\\sqrt{k^2 + 1}}{k} \\), as calculated earlier.\n\nSimilarly, \\( x_A + x_B \\) can be calculated from the expressions of \\( x_1 \\) and \\( x_2 \\):\n\n\\( x_A + x_B = \\frac{p(k^2 + 2 + 2\\sqrt{k^2 + 1})}{2k^2} + \\frac{p(k^2 + 2 - 2\\sqrt{k^2 + 1})}{2k^2} = \\frac{p(k^2 + 2 + 2\\sqrt{k^2 + 1} + k^2 + 2 - 2\\sqrt{k^2 + 1})}{2k^2} \\)\n\nSimplify numerator:\n\n\\( (k^2 + 2 + k^2 + 2) + (2\\sqrt{k^2 + 1} - 2\\sqrt{k^2 + 1}) = 2k^2 + 4 \\)\n\nTherefore, \\( x_A + x_B = \\frac{p(2k^2 + 4)}{2k^2} = \\frac{p(k^2 + 2)}{k^2} \\)\n\nSimilarly, \\( y_A + y_B = \\frac{p}{k}(1 + \\sqrt{k^2 + 1}) + \\frac{p}{k}(1 - \\sqrt{k^2 + 1}) = \\frac{p}{k}(2) = \\frac{2p}{k} \\)\n\nSo substituting back into the equation:\n\n\\( (x_A - x_B)(-2x + x_A + x_B) + (y_A - y_B)(-2y + y_A + y_B) = 0 \\)\n\nPlugging in the known values:\n\nFirst term:\n\n\\( \\frac{2p\\sqrt{k^2 + 1}}{k^2} \\cdot (-2x + \\frac{p(k^2 + 2)}{k^2}) \\)\n\nSecond term:\n\n\\( \\frac{2p\\sqrt{k^2 + 1}}{k} \\cdot (-2y + \\frac{2p}{k}) \\)\n\nTherefore, the entire equation becomes:\n\n\\( \\frac{2p\\sqrt{k^2 + 1}}{k^2} \\cdot \\left(-2x + \\frac{p(k^2 + 2)}{k^2}\\right) + \\frac{2p\\sqrt{k^2 + 1}}{k} \\cdot \\left(-2y + \\frac{2p}{k}\\right) = 0 \\)\n\nFactor out \\( 2p\\sqrt{k^2 + 1} \\):\n\n\\( 2p\\sqrt{k^2 + 1} \\left[ \\frac{1}{k^2} \\left(-2x + \\frac{p(k^2 + 2)}{k^2}\\right) + \\frac{1}{k} \\left(-2y + \\frac{2p}{k}\\right) \\right] = 0 \\)\n\nSince \\( p > 0 \\) and \\( \\sqrt{k^2 + 1} > 0 \\), the only way this product is zero is if the expression in the brackets is zero:\n\n\\( \\frac{1}{k^2} \\left(-2x + \\frac{p(k^2 + 2)}{k^2}\\right) + \\frac{1}{k} \\left(-2y + \\frac{2p}{k}\\right) = 0 \\)\n\nMultiply through by \\( k^2 \\) to eliminate denominators:\n\n\\( -2x + \\frac{p(k^2 + 2)}{k^2} + k(-2y + \\frac{2p}{k}) = 0 \\)\n\nSimplify each term:\n\nFirst term: \\( -2x \\)\n\nSecond term: \\( \\frac{p(k^2 + 2)}{k^2} \\)\n\nThird term: \\( -2ky \\)\n\nFourth term: \\( 2p \\)\n\nTherefore, combining:\n\n\\( -2x + \\frac{p(k^2 + 2)}{k^2} - 2ky + 2p = 0 \\)\n\nCombine the constant terms:\n\n\\( \\frac{p(k^2 + 2)}{k^2} + 2p = \\frac{p(k^2 + 2) + 2p k^2}{k^2} = \\frac{p(k^2 + 2 + 2k^2)}{k^2} = \\frac{p(3k^2 + 2)}{k^2} \\)\n\nTherefore, the equation beco