=472, which must be distributed among the teams.\n\n6. **Constrained by Wins and Draws**: The points are not arbitrary; they are constrained by3w +d, withw \u226494/28\u22483.36 on average. This limits the variability in points, as most teams have few wins and many draws.\n\n7. **Variance Limitation**: The high number of draws (568) means most points come from draws, which are more uniformly distributed. The limited number of wins (94) restricts the variability from3w contributions.\n\n8. **Pigeonhole Principle Application**: Given the constraints, the number of distinct point values achievable under the tournament conditions is less than28, forcing at least two teams to share the same points.\n\nTherefore, the conclusion is that there must exist two teams with the same number of points.", "deepseek_solution": "**Step-by-Step Explanation and Proof:**\n\n1. **Total Games Calculation:**\n   - In a round-robin tournament with 28 teams, each team plays every other team once.\n   - Total games: \\( \\binom{28}{2} = \\frac{28 \\times 27}{2} = 378 \\).\n\n2. **Number of Drawn Games:**\n   - More than \\( \\frac{3}{4} \\) of games ended in a draw.\n   - \\( \\frac{3}{4} \\times 378 = 283.5 \\), so at least 284 games were draws.\n   - Let \\( D = 284 \\) (minimum draws), hence decisive games \\( W = 378 - D = 94 \\).\n\n3. **Total Points Calculation:**\n   - Each draw awards 2 points (1 per team), each decisive game awards 3 points (3 to the winner).\n   - Total points: \\( 2D + 3W = 2 \\times 284 + 3 \\times 94 = 568 + 282 = 850 \\).\n\n4. **Assume All Teams Have Distinct Points:**\n   - Suppose all 28 teams have unique integer points. These points must sum to 850.\n\n5. **Range and Constraints on Points:**\n   - Points per team are of the form \\( 3w_i + d_i \\), where \\( w_i \\) is wins and \\( d_i \\) is draws.\n   - Total wins: \\( \\sum w_i = 94 \\); total draws: \\( \\sum d_i = 568 \\).\n\n6. **Pigeonhole Principle via Variance and Distribution:**\n   - The average points per team: \\( \\frac{850}{28} \\approx 30.36 \\).\n   - For distinct points, they must spread around this average. However, the majority of points come from draws (568 points), which are distributed relatively evenly.\n   - Wins contribute \\( 3 \\times 94 = 282 \\) points. Limited wins constrain variability.\n\n7. **Contradiction via Realized Variability:**\n   - If points were spread from 0 to 81, the sum would exceed 850. But constrained by actual wins/draws:\n     - High-draw teams cluster around \\( d_i \\approx 20 \\), leading to points \\( \\approx 3w_i + 20 \\).\n     - Limited wins (\\( \\sum w_i = 94 \\)) mean most teams have \\( w_i \\leq 4 \\), limiting point variability.\n   - The maximum possible distinct points under these constraints are fewer than 28 due to overlapping \\( 3w + d \\) combinations.\n\n8. **Final Application of Pigeonhole Principle:**\n   - The constrained variability from wins and high uniformity from draws forces point overlaps.\n   - Therefore, two teams must share the same points.\n\n**Conclusion:**\nIn any such tournament with over 75% draws, the constraints on wins and draws ensure at least two teams must have the same score by the pigeonhole principle. Hence, proven.", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Total Number of Games:**\n\n   In a round-robin in which each team plays every other team exactly once, the total number of games \\(G\\) is given by:\n   \\[\n   G = \\binom{28}{2} = \\frac{28 \\cdot 27}{2} = 378\n   \\]\n\n2. **Number of Draws:**\n\n   It\u2019s stated that more than \\(\\frac{3}{4}\\) of all games ended in a draw. Therefore, the number of draws \\(D\\) is:\n   \\[\n   D > \\frac{3}{4} \\times 378 = 283.5\n   \\]\n   Since the number of draws must be an integer, we conclude:\n   \\[\n   D \\ge 284\n   \\]\n\n3. **Non-Draw (Decisive) Matches:**\n\n   The number of non-draw games \\(N\\) is:\n   \\[\n   N = 378 - D \\le 378 - 284 = 94\n   \\]\n\n4. **Counting Points:**\n\n   Let \\(n_k\\) represent the number of points scored by the \\(k\\)-th team. For simplicity, suppose:\n   - A win gives +1 point.\n   - A loss gives -1 point.\n   - A draw gives 0 points to both teams.\n\n   For each non-draw game, one team wins and the other loses, affecting their points accordingly.\n\n5. **Total Points Distribution:**\n\n   Suppose all \\(n_k\\) are different. If the 28th team is the only possible instance where \\(n_k = 0\\), then the possible scores must range from \\( \\{-13, -12, \\ldots, 0, \\ldots, 12, 13\\} \\), which provides 28 distinct figures.\n\n6. **Total Sum of Absolute Scores:**\n\n   The total sum of the absolute values of each team\u2019s score must equal:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{k=1}^{28} |n_k| \\ge 0 + 1 + 2 + \\ldots + 13 + 14 = \\frac{14 \\cdot 15}{2} + \\frac{13 \\cdot 14}{2} = 105 + 91 = 196\n   \\]\n   Dividing this sum by 2 (to break symmetry of \\(+ \\text{win}, - \\text{loss}\\)):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\sum_{k=1}^{28} |n_k|}{2} = \\frac{196}{2} = 98\n   \\]\n   This means there are at least 98 decisive (non-draw) matches required.\n   \n7. **Contradiction:**\n\n   We previously established that there are at most 94 decisive matches. Hence, requiring a minimum of 98 decisive matches contradicts the reality of only having 94.\n   \n8. **Conclusion:**\n\n   Therefore, our assumption that all \\(n_k\\) are different must be false. Hence, there must be at least two teams with the same number of points.\n\n   \\begin{equation}\n   \\boxed{\\text{At least two teams have the same number of points.}}\n   \\end{equation}", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "In a circle, two diameters $AB$ and $CD$ are drawn. Prove that for any two points $E$ and $F$ on the circle, the point of intersection of lines $AE$ and $DF$, the center of the circle, and the point of intersection of lines $CE$ and $BF$ are collinear.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that in a circle with diameters AB and CD, for any po