 at (a, b) and point B at (-a, b) so that AC = BC. Then the midpoint of AB is at (0, b), so the perpendicular bisector of AB is the y-axis. Then, point C is at (0,0). The circle passing through A, B, and C would have its center somewhere on the perpendicular bisector of AB and AC. Hmm, maybe this is getting complicated. Alternatively, maybe using complex numbers could help.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using geometric transformations. Since AC = BC, perhaps reflecting point P over the perpendicular bisector of AB might lead to some symmetry. The perpendicular bisector of AB in the coordinate system I mentioned earlier would be the y-axis if A is (a,0) and B is (-a,0). So reflecting over the y-axis swaps A and B. If I can find a relation between PA and PB through this reflection, maybe that could lead to something.\n\nAlternatively, since D is the foot of the perpendicular from C to PB, which is in triangle PBC. So in triangle PBC, CD is the altitude from C to PB. Therefore, PD is the segment from P to D, where D is on PB. So PD is part of PB. Wait, PD is from P to D, which is on PB, so PD is a subsegment of PB? Wait, no. If D is the foot from C to PB, then D is a point on PB such that CD is perpendicular to PB. So PD is the length from P to D along PB. So PB can be expressed as PD + DB. Therefore, PB = PD + DB. But how does PA come into play here?\n\nWait, the problem states PA + PB = 2PD. So PA + PB equals twice PD. So if I can express PA in terms of PD and DB, maybe. But not sure yet.\n\nAlternatively, maybe consider triangle PCD. Since CD is perpendicular to PB, triangle PCD is a right triangle with right angle at D. So by the Pythagorean theorem, PC\u00b2 = PD\u00b2 + CD\u00b2. Similarly, in triangle CDB, CB\u00b2 = CD\u00b2 + DB\u00b2. Since AC = BC, which is given. So CB = AC. Hmm.\n\nWait, since APBC is cyclic, points A, P, B, C lie on a circle. So angles subtended by the same chord are equal. For example, angle APC equals angle ABC because they subtend arc AC. Wa